<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Minh Hương</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Minh Huong</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Minh Hương were descendants of Chinese, many of them Ming loyalists, who settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> after the fall of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ming-empire">Ming dynasty</a> in 1644. Registered as a separate community, they intermarried with Vietnamese, adopted local customs and served as merchants, officials and scholars under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nguyen-lords">Nguyen lords</a> and emperors. The scholar-official Trịnh Hoài Đức was among them.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/minh-huong.jpg" alt="Minh Hương" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bùi Thụy Đào Nguyên · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:B%C3%AAn_trong_%C4%90%C3%ACnh_Minh_H%C6%B0%C6%A1ng_Gia_Th%E1%BA%A1nh.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Minh Hương were among the first ethnic Han to settle in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>. Some 3,000 Ming refugees, fiercely loyal to the fallen dynasty and opposed to the Qing, came to Vietnam at the end of the Ming. Most were soldiers and single men, and they married Vietnamese women. Their descendants identified strongly as Han despite their Vietnamese mothers, and unlike later Han migrants of the Qing period they did not wear the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchus">Manchu</a> hairstyle.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>After the Qing conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> proper, Han leaders who refused to submit, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mac-cuu">Mạc Cửu</a>, Trần Thượng Xuyên and Dương Ngạn Địch, reached the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mekong-delta">Mekong Delta</a> and set up polities of their own at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ha-tien">Hà Tiên</a>, Biên Hòa and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/my-tho">Mỹ Tho</a>. They later submitted to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nguyen-lords">Nguyễn lords</a>, who gave them noble titles and protection against threats from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-empire">Khmer</a> and Siam. Many Minh Hương, such as Trịnh Hoài Đức and Ngô Nhân Tịnh, became ministers of the Nguyễn and took an important part in the exploration and settlement of the Mekong Delta.</p><p>Their name was first written with characters meaning those who worship Ming ancestors, and in 1827 the Minh Mạng Emperor ordered it written with characters meaning of Ming origin. In the records of the Nguyễn dynasty they were called Minh nhân or Minh Hương, to set them apart from the Thanh nhân, Han people from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing China</a>. From 1829 they were treated as Vietnamese rather than Han, and they were not allowed to travel to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> or to wear the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchus">Manchu</a> queue.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The Minh Hương often married Viet people, and most have adopted Vietnamese culture. Unlike later waves of Han immigrants, they are counted by the Vietnamese government as Kinh rather than Hoa, and they overwhelmingly identify themselves as Kinh. Among those of Minh Hương descent were the rulers of the Principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ha-tien">Hà Tiên</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mac-cuu">Mạc Cửu</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mac-thien-tu">Mạc Thiên Tứ</a>, the 18th-century scholar Võ Trường Toản, and Phan Thanh Giản and Trần Tiễn Thành, mandarins in the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tu-duc">Tự Đức</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Minh Hương” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Chinese merchants, including the <b>Minh Huong</b> descendants of Ming loyalists, handled much of the commerce, while Western ships were rarely welcomed.</blockquote><a href="/empire/nguyen-dynasty">Nguyen dynasty · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
